-- R4a — ATTENUATION CHAINS: a non-root holder can narrow further (2026-08-06).
--
-- `cap_narrow` was typed `Cap(IO) -> Cap(a)`: its argument was LITERALLY the
-- root. So a holder of anything narrower could not attenuate, and this file
-- was an error before R4a:
--
--     expected `IO` but got `IO.FileSystem`
--
-- Delegation with attenuation at each hop — hand a subsystem
-- `Cap(IO.FileSystem)`, let it hand a helper `Cap(IO.FileRead)` — is the core
-- object-capability discipline, and it was not expressible. Every narrowing
-- had to happen up at `main` with the already-narrowed values threaded down.
--
-- That matters now rather than later because R2 made `main` the sole source of
-- authority, and R1 will make every IO call need a token: "least privilege,
-- threaded down" becomes the normal way to write March, and it was the one
-- idiom the typing forbade.
--
-- Read with reject/t153 (widening), reject/t154 (siblings) and reject/t155
-- (the deferred case). This file alone cannot tell a working subsumption
-- check from one that permits everything.
mod ChainedAttenuation do
  needs IO

  -- The middle hop: holds FileSystem, hands out something strictly narrower.
  pfn reader_from(fs : Cap(IO.FileSystem)) : Cap(IO.FileRead) do
    cap_narrow(fs)
  end

  -- Identity: a capability subsumes ITSELF, so narrowing to the same level is
  -- legal. `cap_subsumes p p` is true, and a check written with a strict
  -- ancestor test would wrongly reject this.
  pfn same_level(r : Cap(IO.FileRead)) : Cap(IO.FileRead) do
    cap_narrow(r)
  end

  fn main(cap : Cap(IO)) do
    let fs : Cap(IO.FileSystem) = cap_narrow(cap)
    let r  = reader_from(fs)
    let r2 = same_level(r)
    println("attenuation chained two hops below the root")
  end
end
